        "title": "Shortest Paths",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Shortest-path algorithms are distinguished almost entirely by their\n**preconditions**, not their mechanics — which is exactly why they are so easy to misapply. Every one of them is built from a single\noperation — *relaxation* — but each makes a different assumption (non-negative\nweights, acyclicity, no negative cycle) that, if violated, makes the algorithm not\nslow but *incorrect*. The decision tree is the whole content."
    },
